Cell["\<\
At fixed \[Gamma] the S_\[ScriptL]m (\[ScriptL] = 2, 3, ...) form an \
orthonormal basis: they are eigenfunctions of a self-adjoint operator. S's at \
different \[Gamma] are not orthogonal to each other. That is exactly why \
expanding one in terms of the other (Y = S at \[Gamma] = 0) gives a \
non-trivial matrix.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8f5a2b5b-2d1d-474c-81e0-f7192bd51f42"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Exercise 1", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"85d25537-82ee-4403-8186-e2b13c75eebc"],

Cell["\<\
(a) Check that SpinWeightedSpheroidalHarmonicS[s, l, 2, 0] equals Y[l, 2, \
\[Theta]] for l = 2..5 at \[Theta] = 0.7. (b) Write an inner product ip[f, g] \
= 2\[Pi] \[Integral] f(\[Theta])* g(\[Theta]) sin\[Theta] d\[Theta] \
(NIntegrate, f and g pure functions of \[Theta]). With it, check \
\:27e8S\:2082\:2082|S\:2082\:2082\:27e9 = 1 and \:27e8S\:2082\:2082|S\:2083\
\:2082\:27e9 = 0 at \[Gamma] = 0.5. (c) Compute \:27e8Y\:2082\:2082|S\:2082\
\:2082(\[Gamma]=0.5)\:27e9 and \
\:27e8Y\:2083\:2082|S\:2082\:2082(\[Gamma]=0.5)\:27e9. These numbers are your \
first two mixing coefficients.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0a4f522c-8edc-42d2-8f88-c687d08bf5d7"],

